  "body": "## Problem\n\nThe README \"Contributing\" section does not tell a contributor how much work fits their AI subscription. A contributor with a small plan starts a large issue, runs out of quota, and the PR stalls half done. Some contributor agents also try to push to `NeoTech/Camcraft` or ask for maintainer-only API reads, instead of the fork flow.\n\n## Change\n\nAdd a subsection `### How you can help, by AI budget` to the README \"Contributing\" section (after \"Tools\"). Write it in ASD-STE100. Content:\n\n1. A table: budget tier -> what to pick -> what to avoid.\n   - No AI subscription: test on a real machine (air or foam cuts), report bugs with the posted program, send the control's programming manual, check docs.\n   - Small plan (about USD 20 a month; a few hours of this project per week): `effort:low` issues (docs, labels, a test fixture, a string); one small PR at a time; reviews of a single small PR.\n   - Medium plan: `effort:medium` issues (one crate, a known pattern).\n   - Large plan or team seats: `effort:high` and `effort:max` issues, reviews, research.\n2. \"If you run out of quota\": open the PR as a draft with what you have, tick \"Allow edits by maintainers\", and comment `help me finish`. Maintainers build, test and finish it.\n3. \"You do not need a big disk\": the review boxes build and test each PR. Run only the tests of your change, or none.\n4. Fork flow reminder, in four lines: work on a branch in your fork; open the PR from that branch into `NeoTech/Camcraft:main`; push review fixes to the same branch; merge `main` into it, never rebase or force-push an open PR. Do not push to `NeoTech/Camcraft`. Ask ownership questions on the PR; a maintainer answers.\n\nDocs only. No code change.\n\nDone means: the README has the subsection with the four parts above, and the existing Contributing text links to it once.\n",
